<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bavarians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Baiuvarii</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bavarians, or Baiuvarii, are a people who first appear in written sources in the mid-6th century, settled between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the Lech and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. They formed from Germanic groups and the Romanised population of the old provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a> and Noricum, under dukes of the Agilolfing family. The Bavarians later expanded east along the Danube and into the eastern Alps, and they gave their name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bavarians.jpg" alt="Bavarians" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: diese Datei: Jwnabd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bavaria_(lozengy).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Baiuvarii is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> form of a Germanic word meaning citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, the Boii home that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> already named at the end of the 1st century, though how the people were connected with that region is uncertain. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontier had long mixed older populations with Roman border troops, Germanic clients and peoples who had once been under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila the Hun</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe-germanic-peoples">Elbe Germans</a> from the north and East Germanic groups such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>. The Bavarians seem to have been closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> to their east, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settled among them in the Upper Palatinate and around Regensburg.</p><p>They appear as the Alamanni and Thuringians fell under Frankish hegemony and the kingdoms of Theoderic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> came to an end, leaving a power vacuum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Their law shows heavy Roman influence, and they seem to have been united under a duke installed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Christian dukes ruled from the 6th century, but the people were converted gradually through the 7th and into the 8th. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corbinian">Saint Corbinian</a>, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ii">Pope Gregory II</a>, became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a>, the see of Lorch moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a>, and Boniface founded the Bishopric of Regensburg in 739. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-baiuvariorum">Lex Baiuvariorum</a> was recorded in the 740s. The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> became a stem duchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in the 10th century, and its eastern march, formed in 976, became the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1156. Divided in the 14th and 15th centuries, Bavaria was reunited by Duke Albrecht IV in 1503, became an Electorate in 1623 and a kingdom at the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> in 1805.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Today Bavarians are an ethnographic group of Germans native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-bavaria">Altbayern</a>, roughly the old Electorate, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a> and the Upper Palatinate; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, joined to the kingdom in the 19th century, are not culturally Bavarian. Like the Austrians, from whom there is no linguistic distinction, they are traditionally Catholic. Their dialects are Upper Palatinian, Danube Bavarian and Alpine Bavarian, the last reaching into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bavarians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Bavarians</b>, or Baiuvarii, first appear in written sources in the mid 6th century, when Jordanes mentions them east of the Swabians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 900 Moravians and <b>Bavarians</b> accused each other of swearing alliances with them “by the means of a dog and a wolf”, and a large Hungarian army “attacked and invaded” Moravia in 900.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Many <b>Bavarians</b>, especially from the Palatinate and Franconia, emigrated to North America in the nineteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Suebi, probably including Marcomanni, went west and founded the Kingdom of the Suebi in Gallaecia; others joined the empire of Attila, the Lombards and the Alemanni, and it is very likely that the <b>Baiuvarii</b> included Marcomanni.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Pribina ruled as a Frankish vassal, settling Slavs and <b>Bavarians</b> and founding some 30 churches consecrated by the archbishops of Salzburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/balaton-principality">Balaton Principality</a></li><li><blockquote>Tyrol remained a Habsburg crown land, fighting the <b>Bavarians</b> and French under Andreas Hofer in 1809.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tyrol">County of Tyrol</a></li></ul></section>
